      "learning_objective_id": "OOP_T00_3_LO02",
      "question_category": "misconception_analysis",
      "stem": "In the C++17 snippet below, `a = 5` and `b = 2` are small `int` values, and `d = 1.0` is a `double`:\n```cpp\n#include <iostream>\n\nint main() {\n    int a = 5;\n    int b = 2;\n    double d = 1.0;\n    double x = d * (a / b);\n    double y = (d * a) / b;\n    std::cout << x << \" \" << y << \"\\n\";\n    return 0;\n}\n```\nWhy does this program print `2 2.5` instead of `2.5 2.5`?",
      "options": [
        {
          "option_id": "A",
          "text": "Because multiplying by `double d = 1.0` converts `d` to `int` whenever `a` and `b` are `int`."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "B",
          "text": "Because `/` only performs floating-point division when the `double` operand appears on the right-hand side of `/`."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "C",
          "text": "Because `int` values such as `5` lose precision when converted to `double` inside parentheses."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "D",
          "text": "Because `(a / b)` has two `int` operands and performs integer division (`2`) before multiplying by `d`, whereas `(d * a)` produces a `double` (`5.0`) so `/ b` converts `b` to `double` and yields `2.5`."
        }
      ],
      "correct_option_id": "D",
      "answer_explanation": "Usual arithmetic conversions apply per operator based on that operator's direct operand types. In `d * (a / b)`, the parenthesized `(a / b)` has two `int` operands (`5` and `2`), so integer division computes `2` first, and only then is `2` converted to `2.0` to multiply `d`. In `(d * a) / b`, `d * a` pairs `double` with `int`, converting `a` to `double` (`5.0`), and then `5.0 / b` converts `b` to `double` (`2.0`) to yield `2.5`.",
      "distractor_explanations": {
        "A": "Usual arithmetic conversions convert the `int` operand to `double`, never `double` to `int`.",
        "B": "If either operand of `/` is `double` (left or right), the other operand is converted to `double` and floating-point division is used.",
        "C": "Small integers like `5` and `2` are represented exactly in `double` without precision loss."
      },
      "difficulty_estimate": "medium",
      "misconception_tags": [
        "per_operator_arithmetic_conversion",
        "parentheses_and_int_division"
      ],
      "authorship_type": "AI-assisted team-authored",
