      "learning_objective_id": "OOP_T01_1_LO01",
      "question_category": "concept_recognition",
      "stem": "Consider the following C++17 code:\n```cpp\nclass Thermostat {\npublic:\n    int target_c;\n    bool eco_mode;\n};\n\nint main() {\n    Thermostat living{22, false};\n    Thermostat bedroom{19, true};\n    return (living.target_c != bedroom.target_c && living.eco_mode != bedroom.eco_mode) ? 0 : 1;\n}\n```\nWhich statement accurately describes `Thermostat`, `living`, and `bedroom`?",
      "options": [
        {
          "option_id": "A",
          "text": "`Thermostat` is a user-defined class type (blueprint), while `living` and `bedroom` are two separate object instances that each store their own `target_c` and `eco_mode` fields."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "B",
          "text": "`Thermostat` is a single runtime object holding shared `target_c` and `eco_mode` values, while `living` and `bedroom` are references to that object."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "C",
          "text": "`living` and `bedroom` are two distinct class definitions, and `Thermostat` is a function that returns both definitions."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "D",
          "text": "Initializing `bedroom{19, true}` overwrites the `target_c` and `eco_mode` fields of `living` because both variables have type `Thermostat`."
        }
      ],
      "correct_option_id": "A",
      "answer_explanation": "In C++, `class Thermostat` defines a user-defined type (blueprint) specifying the members `target_c` and `eco_mode`. `living` and `bedroom` are two distinct objects (instances) of `Thermostat`, and each allocates its own non-static data members initialized independently (`{22, false}` and `{19, true}`).",
      "distractor_explanations": {
        "B": "`Thermostat` is a type definition, not a runtime instance, and declaring `living` and `bedroom` creates two separate objects rather than aliases.",
        "C": "`Thermostat` is the class type definition; `living` and `bedroom` are variable definitions of object instances inside `main()`, not class definitions.",
        "D": "Each instance allocates its own non-static data members in memory, so initializing `bedroom` leaves `living.target_c == 22` and `living.eco_mode == false` unchanged."
      },
      "difficulty_estimate": "easy",
