      "learning_objective_id": "OOP_T02_2_LO01",
      "question_category": "misconception_analysis",
      "stem": "Given `Player p1{10}; Player p2{10};` where `struct Player { int score; void gain(int pts) { score += pts; } };`, a student calls `p1.gain(5);` and predicts both `p1.score` and `p2.score` become `15`. Why is that prediction wrong?",
      "options": [
        {
          "option_id": "A",
          "text": "Because `gain` lacks a reference `&` on `pts`, preventing `p1.score` from updating."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "B",
          "text": "Because `p1` and `p2` hold separate `score` state, and `p1.gain(5)` acts only on `p1`."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "C",
          "text": "Because `Player` is a `struct`, so `p1.gain(5)` has no effect unless `class` is used."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "D",
          "text": "Because invoking `p1.gain(5)` overwrites `p2.score` with `5` instead of adding to it."
        }
      ],
      "correct_option_id": "B",
      "answer_explanation": "Non-static data members belong to each individual instance, and a non-static member function call `p1.gain(5)` binds its implicit target object (`*this`) strictly to `p1`. Therefore `p1.score` becomes `15` while `p2.score` remains `10`.",
      "distractor_explanations": {
        "A": "`pts` is an input value (`5`) added to `p1`'s own `score` member; `pts` does not need `&` to update `p1.score`.",
        "C": "`struct` and `class` follow identical member-function dispatch rules in C++; `p1.gain(5)` updates `p1.score` either way.",
        "D": "`p1.gain(5)` does not touch `p2` at all; `p2.score` remains `10`."
      },
      "difficulty_estimate": "easy",
      "misconception_tags": [
        "per_instance_member_update"
      ],
