      "correct_option_id": "B",
      "answer_explanation": "In a declaration `Point p2 = p1;`, `p2` does not yet exist; the `=` token denotes copy-initialization, which constructs `p2` directly via the copy constructor without calling `operator=`.",
      "distractor_explanations": {
        "A": "`Point p2 = p1;` is valid C++ copy-initialization syntax (provided the copy constructor is accessible and not `explicit`).",
        "C": "`Point p2` declares a distinct object variable, not a reference (`Point& p2 = p1;`).",
        "D": "Assignment (`operator=`) applies to an already-existing object on the left-hand side (e.g., `p2 = p1;`), regardless of whether `Point` has `const` methods."
      },
      "difficulty_estimate": "easy",
