      "topic_id": "OOP_T03_2",
      "learning_objective_id": "OOP_T03_2_LO02",
      "question_category": "misconception_analysis",
      "stem": "Consider the following C++17 class with the stated ownership rule:\n```cpp\nclass IntResource {\nprivate:\n    int* data_; // Ownership rule: each IntResource exclusively owns data_\npublic:\n    IntResource(int v) : data_(new int(v)) {}\n    ~IntResource() { delete data_; }\n    // No custom copy constructor declared\n};\n```\nSuppose a function creates `IntResource a(5);` and then writes `IntResource b = a;`. Given the ownership rule, why does this copy break exclusive ownership?",
      "options": [
        {
          "option_id": "A",
          "text": "Because `IntResource` defines `~IntResource()`, the compiler automatically performs an expensive deep copy for `b = a`, creating redundant allocations."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "B",
          "text": "The default copy constructor shallow-copies `data_`'s address, causing both destructors to delete the same shared memory at end of scope."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "C",
          "text": "The default copy constructor directs `b.data_` to point to object `a` rather than the dynamically allocated int, corrupting member state."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "D",
          "text": "C++ access rules prohibit copy-initialization of `b` from `a` whenever a user-declared destructor is defined without an explicit assignment operator."
        }
      ],
      "correct_option_id": "B",
      "answer_explanation": "Because no custom copy constructor is declared, the compiler generates a memberwise copy that copies `a.data_`'s address into `b.data_`. Both `a` and `b` now store the same address and both `~IntResource()` destructors assume exclusive ownership—leaving `a` with a dangling pointer once `b` deletes the shared `int`, and causing a conflicting second `delete` when `a` is destroyed.",
      "distractor_explanations": {
        "A": "Defining a destructor does NOT make the compiler synthesize a deep copy; the implicit copy constructor still performs a shallow memberwise pointer copy.",
        "C": "`b.data_` receives the address stored inside `a.data_` (the heap `int`), not `&a`.",
        "D": "In C++17, defining a destructor does not delete the implicitly-declared copy constructor (though it is deprecated if implicit copy is used); the shallow copy still compiles and causes the ownership conflict."
      },
      "difficulty_estimate": "medium",
      "misconception_tags": [
        "shallow_copy_double_ownership",
        "owning_raw_pointer"
      ],
      "authorship_type": "AI-assisted team-authored",
      "reference_sources": [
        "https://en.cppreference.com/cpp/language/copy_constructor#Implicitly-defined_copy_constructor",
        "https://www.learncpp.com/cpp-tutorial/shallow-vs-deep-copying/",
        "https://isocpp.github.io/CppCoreGuidelines/CppCoreGuidelines#c33-if-a-class-has-an-owning-pointer-member-define-a-destructor"
      ],
      "verification_method": "source_cross_check_and_cpp17_compile",
      "agent_check_status": "passed",
      "human_review_status": "pending",
