      "correct_option_id": "B",
      "answer_explanation": "Under `public` inheritance, `public` members of `Base` (`secret()`) are accessible in `Derived`, but `private` members of `Base` (`secret_`) remain private to `Base` and cannot be accessed directly by name in `Derived`.",
      "distractor_explanations": {
        "A": "A `Derived` object does contain the `Base` subobject (including `secret_`); the restriction is access control, not missing storage.",
        "C": "A `const` member function can read ordinary accessible data members; the error comes from `secret_` being `private` in `Base`.",
        "D": "Declaring `int secret_;` inside `Derived` would create a separate shadowed member rather than accessing `Base::secret_`."
      },
      "difficulty_estimate": "easy",
