      "question_id": "Q_OOP_T04_1_08",
      "question_family_id": "QF_OOP_T04_1_F08",
      "concept_id": "OOP_C04",
      "topic_id": "OOP_T04_1",
      "learning_objective_id": "OOP_T04_1_LO02",
      "question_category": "short_application",
      "stem": "You are modeling three classes for a billing app:\n- `Document`: stores `title` and `author`.\n- `Invoice`: a specialized document with `invoice_no` and `total_cents`.\n- `Printer`: a separate device class that prints a `Document`.\nWhich design choice accurately applies `is-a` inheritance?",
      "options": [
        {
          "option_id": "A",
          "text": "Make both `Invoice` and `Printer` inherit publicly from `Document` so that `Printer` can access `title` directly through base class inheritance."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "B",
          "text": "Make `Document` inherit publicly from `Invoice` and require `Printer` to maintain an explicit `Document` pointer to print records accurately."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "C",
          "text": "Make `Invoice` derive publicly from `Document` (is-a), while keeping `Printer` outside the hierarchy (`Printer` merely acts on documents)."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "D",
          "text": "Avoid inheritance entirely for all three classes because `Invoice` references `Document` fields, indicating a pure has-a relationship."
        }
      ],
      "correct_option_id": "C",
      "answer_explanation": "An `Invoice` is a specialized `Document` (`is-a`), so `class Invoice : public Document` is appropriate. A `Printer` is not a `Document` (it merely acts on/uses a `Document`), so `Printer` must not inherit from `Document`.",
      "distractor_explanations": {
        "A": "A `Printer` is not a `Document`; inheriting from `Document` just to access `title` violates `is-a`.",
        "B": "Making `Document` inherit from `Invoice` reverses the general-to-specific hierarchy (every `Document` is not an `Invoice`).",
        "D": "Sharing general `Document` attributes in a specialized `Invoice` is a classic `is-a` relationship, not merely `has-a`."
      },
      "difficulty_estimate": "medium",
      "misconception_tags": [
        "is_a_modeling_decision",
        "hierarchy_boundary"
      ],
      "authorship_type": "AI-assisted team-authored",
