      "learning_objective_id": "OOP_T04_2_LO02",
      "question_category": "concept_recognition",
      "stem": "In a derived class, what is the purpose of writing the `override` specifier at the end of a member function declaration such as `void draw() const override;`?",
      "options": [
        {
          "option_id": "A",
          "text": "It is required syntax to enable virtual dispatch; without `override`, the base class method is always called through a base reference."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "B",
          "text": "It instructs the compiler to verify that the function is virtual and overrides a matching virtual function in a base class, producing a compile error otherwise."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "C",
          "text": "It allows a derived method with a completely different name to replace any virtual method in the base class."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "D",
          "text": "It converts a non-virtual base class function into a virtual function retroactively."
        }
      ],
      "correct_option_id": "B",
      "answer_explanation": "In C++11 and later, `override` ensures that the derived function is virtual and overrides a virtual function from a base class with a matching signature. If the base method is missing, non-virtual, or has a different name/signature, the program is ill-formed and fails to compile.",
      "distractor_explanations": {
        "A": "`override` is optional for overriding: if the base function is `virtual` and the derived signature matches, overriding occurs even without `override`.",
        "C": "Overriding requires the exact same function name, parameter types, and cv/ref-qualifiers; `override` rejects a mismatched name.",
        "D": "`override` cannot make a non-virtual base function virtual; trying to `override` a non-virtual base function causes a compile-time error."
      },
