      "learning_objective_id": "OOP_T04_2_LO02",
      "question_category": "misconception_analysis",
      "stem": "A developer expects `FileLogger::logFile(int)` to override `Logger::log(int)` so `ref.log(3)` prints `File:3`. Instead, the C++17 program prints `Base:3` followed by `File:3`:\n```cpp\n#include <iostream>\n\nclass Logger {\npublic:\n    virtual void log(int level) const { std::cout << \"Base:\" << level << \"\\n\"; }\n};\n\nclass FileLogger : public Logger {\npublic:\n    void logFile(int level) const { std::cout << \"File:\" << level << \"\\n\"; }\n};\n\nint main() {\n    FileLogger fl;\n    const Logger& ref = fl;\n    ref.log(3);\n    fl.logFile(3);\n    return 0;\n}\n```\nWhy does `ref.log(3)` call `Logger::log(int)` instead of `FileLogger::logFile(int)`?",
      "options": [
        {
          "option_id": "A",
          "text": "`logFile` is an unrelated method name that does not match `Logger::log`, so `FileLogger` inherits `Logger::log` unchanged without overriding it (and adding `override` to `logFile` would fail to compile)."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "B",
          "text": "`Logger::log` is marked `const`, which prevents any derived method from overriding it unless `const` is removed."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "C",
          "text": "`ref` is a `const Logger&` reference rather than a `Logger*` pointer, and virtual dispatch in C++ only works through pointers."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "D",
          "text": "`FileLogger::logFile` lacks the `virtual` keyword at the front, which is mandatory in the derived class to override `Logger::log`."
        }
      ],
      "correct_option_id": "A",
      "answer_explanation": "To override a virtual function in a base class, the derived member function must have the exact same name as well as the same parameter types and cv-qualifiers. Because `logFile` is a different, unrelated method name from `log`, `FileLogger` does not override `Logger::log(int)` (and adding `override` to `logFile` would produce a compile error). Thus `ref.log(3)` calls `Logger::log(3)` (`Base:3`), while `fl.logFile(3)` calls `FileLogger::logFile(3)` (`File:3`).",
      "distractor_explanations": {
        "B": "`const` virtual functions can be overridden normally as long as the derived override uses the same name, parameter list, and `const` qualifier.",
        "C": "Virtual dispatch works identically through both base references (`const Logger&`) and base pointers (`const Logger*`).",
        "D": "Repeating `virtual` in the derived class is optional; the reason `logFile` does not override `log` is that its method name does not match `log`."
      },
