      "correct_option_id": "C",
      "answer_explanation": "Adding the pure-specifier `= 0` to `virtual double area() const = 0;` declares a pure virtual function. Any class that defines or inherits at least one un-overridden pure virtual function is an abstract class, so objects of type `Shape` cannot be created directly.",
      "distractor_explanations": {
        "A": "`= 0` is the pure-specifier syntax marking a pure virtual function, not a return value `0.0`; abstract classes cannot be instantiated.",
        "B": "`sides()` is a non-virtual concrete function whose body happens to return `0`; pure virtual functions use `= 0` on a `virtual` declarator.",
        "D": "Although objects of an abstract class cannot be instantiated, pointers (`Shape*`) and references (`Shape&`) to an abstract class are explicitly allowed."
      },
      "difficulty_estimate": "easy",
      "misconception_tags": [
        "pure_virtual_recognition",
        "abstract_instantiation_rule"
      ],
      "authorship_type": "AI-assisted team-authored",
