      "topic_id": "OOP_T04_3",
      "learning_objective_id": "OOP_T04_3_LO02",
      "question_category": "short_application",
      "stem": "In the C++17 snippet below, `delete p;` deletes a `FilePlugin` object through a `Plugin*` base pointer:\n```cpp\nclass Plugin {\npublic:\n    // <-- choose destructor here\n    virtual void run() = 0;\n};\n\nclass FilePlugin : public Plugin {\npublic:\n    void run() override {}\n    ~FilePlugin() override {}\n};\n\nint main() {\n    Plugin* p = new FilePlugin();\n    p->run();\n    delete p;\n    return 0;\n}\n```\nWhich destructor declaration belongs in `Plugin` so that `~FilePlugin()` can `override` it and `delete p;` is well-defined?",
      "options": [
        {
          "option_id": "A",
          "text": "`virtual ~Plugin() = default;`"
        },
        {
          "option_id": "B",
          "text": "`~Plugin() = default;`"
        },
        {
          "option_id": "C",
          "text": "`void ~Plugin() {}`"
        },
        {
          "option_id": "D",
          "text": "`virtual void destroy() = 0;`"
        }
      ],
      "correct_option_id": "A",
      "answer_explanation": "`virtual ~Plugin() = default;` declares a public virtual destructor on the base class `Plugin`. This makes `~FilePlugin() override` valid and ensures `delete p;` dispatches dynamically to `~FilePlugin()` and then `~Plugin()` without undefined behavior.",
      "distractor_explanations": {
        "B": "Without `virtual`, `~Plugin()` is non-virtual, so `~FilePlugin() override` fails to compile and deleting via `Plugin*` would be undefined behavior.",
        "C": "Destructors cannot specify a return type (not even `void`), and this declaration also lacks `virtual`.",
        "D": "Adding an ordinary `destroy()` method is not a destructor; `delete p;` invokes the destructor `~Plugin()`, not `destroy()`."
      },
      "difficulty_estimate": "easy",
