      "question_id": "Q_OOP_T05_1_07",
      "question_family_id": "QF_OOP_T05_1_F07",
      "concept_id": "OOP_C05",
      "topic_id": "OOP_T05_1",
      "learning_objective_id": "OOP_T05_1_LO01",
      "question_category": "misconception_analysis",
      "stem": "Consider the following C++17 classes with explicit ownership comments:\n```cpp\nclass CatalogItem {\npublic:\n    int sku;\n    CatalogItem(int s) : sku(s) {}\n};\n\nclass CartItem {\nprivate:\n    const CatalogItem* item_; // Association: non-owning pointer to store catalog\n    int quantity_;\npublic:\n    CartItem(const CatalogItem* item, int qty)\n        : item_(item), quantity_(qty) {}\n    int sku() const { return item_ ? item_->sku : -1; }\n    int qty() const { return quantity_; }\n};\n\nclass ShoppingCart {\nprivate:\n    CartItem primary_; // Composition: owned by ShoppingCart\npublic:\n    ShoppingCart(const CatalogItem* item, int qty)\n        : primary_(item, qty) {}\n    int primary_sku() const { return primary_.sku(); }\n    int primary_qty() const { return primary_.qty(); }\n};\n```\nA student claims: *\"Destroying a `ShoppingCart` must also `delete` the `CatalogItem` pointed to by `primary_`, and two `ShoppingCart`s cannot point to the same `CatalogItem`.\"* Which analysis accurately corrects this misconception?",
      "options": [
        {
          "option_id": "A",
          "text": "The student is right that two `ShoppingCart`s cannot point to the same `CatalogItem`, because raw pointers always enforce exclusive `1`-to-`1` composition."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "B",
          "text": "Destroying `ShoppingCart` destroys its owned `CartItem` (`primary_`), not the associated `CatalogItem`; multiple `CartItem`s across different `ShoppingCart`s can share a pointer to the same `CatalogItem`, provided the caller keeps that `CatalogItem` alive longer than any `ShoppingCart` pointing to it."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "C",
          "text": "`CartItem` owns `CatalogItem` because `CartItem` receives `const CatalogItem*` in its constructor, whereas `ShoppingCart` only has an association with `CartItem`."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "D",
          "text": "If the `CatalogItem` is destroyed before `ShoppingCart`, `item_` automatically resets to `nullptr`, so calling `primary_sku()` safely returns `-1`."
        }
      ],
      "correct_option_id": "B",
      "answer_explanation": "`ShoppingCart` composes `CartItem` by value (managing `primary_`'s lifetime), whereas `CartItem` only holds a non-owning association pointer (`const CatalogItem*`) to an externally managed `CatalogItem`. In an association, multiple objects can point to the same `CatalogItem`, destroying `ShoppingCart` leaves `CatalogItem` intact, and the caller must ensure `CatalogItem` outlives any `ShoppingCart` referencing it.",
      "distractor_explanations": {
        "A": "Non-owning pointers are a standard way to implement associations (`uses-a`), where the associated object can be shared by multiple objects simultaneously.",
        "C": "This reverses both relations: `ShoppingCart` owns `CartItem` by value (composition), while `CartItem` merely points to an externally owned `CatalogItem` (association).",
        "D": "C++ raw pointers do not automatically become `nullptr` when the target object is destroyed; reading through a dangling pointer causes undefined behavior."
      },
      "difficulty_estimate": "medium",
      "misconception_tags": [
        "pointer_implies_ownership",
        "association_sharing_and_lifetime",
        "dangling_pointer_lifetime"
      ],
      "authorship_type": "AI-assisted team-authored",
      "reference_sources": [
        "https://www.learncpp.com/cpp-tutorial/composition/",
        "https://www.learncpp.com/cpp-tutorial/association/"
      ],
      "verification_method": "source_cross_check_and_cpp17_compile",
      "agent_check_status": "passed",
      "human_review_status": "pending",
      "approved_by": null,
      "approved_at": null,
      "reviewer_comment": "",
      "version": "1.0.0"
