      "learning_objective_id": "OOP_T05_2_LO01",
      "question_category": "misconception_analysis",
      "stem": "A developer says: \"Because an account balance is private, every text-formatting function must be a member of `Account`.\" `Account` already has a public `balance()` getter, and formatting may use it. Which response is most accurate?",
      "options": [
        {
          "option_id": "A",
          "text": "A free helper can call `balance()`; private data still allows non-member formatters."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "B",
          "text": "Every function reading `Account` data must be a member to preserve encapsulation."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "C",
          "text": "An external formatting helper must be a `friend` of `Account` to call `balance()`."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "D",
          "text": "Making `balance` public is required whenever external functions format an `Account`."
        }
      ],
      "correct_option_id": "A",
      "answer_explanation": "The public getter is a sufficient interface for this helper. Keeping private state does not require every operation that uses a public value to be a member.",
      "distractor_explanations": {
        "B": "Students often equate encapsulation with putting every helper inside the class, yet calling public `balance()` preserves encapsulation without bloating `Account`.",
        "C": "Learners sometimes add `friend` for any non-member helper, but `friend` is only for direct private access and is unnecessary when `balance()` is public.",
        "D": "Exposing mutable fields feels tempting for external printing, yet `Account`'s existing public getter already supplies the value safely without breaking encapsulation."
      },
