      "question_id": "Q_OOP_T05_2_07",
      "question_family_id": "QF_OOP_T05_2_F07",
      "concept_id": "OOP_C05",
      "topic_id": "OOP_T05_2",
      "learning_objective_id": "OOP_T05_2_LO02",
      "question_category": "short_application",
      "stem": "This C++17 code prints `print:12` by having `PrintQueue` delegate to a `Printer` member rather than using `class PrintQueue : public Printer`. A `PrintQueue` organizes waiting jobs and cannot act as a hardware `Printer` (it must not expose `calibrate()` as a `Printer`). Why is this composition design better?\n```cpp\n#include <iostream>\n\nclass Printer {\npublic:\n    void print_job(int id) const { std::cout << \"print:\" << id << \"\\n\"; }\n    void calibrate() const { std::cout << \"calibrate\\n\"; }\n};\n\nclass PrintQueue {\nprivate:\n    Printer printer_;\n    int next_id_;\npublic:\n    PrintQueue(int id) : next_id_(id) {}\n    void flush_next() { printer_.print_job(next_id_); }\n};\n\nint main() {\n    PrintQueue q(12);\n    q.flush_next();\n}\n```",
      "options": [
        {
          "option_id": "A",
          "text": "Because `PrintQueue` can only call `print_job()` on a `Printer` when `Printer` is a member, never via inheritance."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "B",
          "text": "Because reversing it to `class Printer : public PrintQueue` would give every `Printer` its own waiting job queue."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "C",
          "text": "Because `PrintQueue` has-a `Printer` to delegate jobs without falsely claiming a queue is-a `Printer`."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "D",
          "text": "Because public inheritance in C++ forbids a derived class from adding its own private `next_id_` data member."
        }
      ],
      "correct_option_id": "C",
      "answer_explanation": "`PrintQueue` has and uses a `Printer` member, so delegating `flush_next()` to `printer_.print_job(next_id_)` models the relationship accurately without exposing `Printer`'s full interface as an `is-a` base.",
      "distractor_explanations": {
        "A": "Students sometimes think inheritance blocks base calls, but public inheritance would expose both `print_job()` and `calibrate()` while wrongly modeling `PrintQueue` as a `Printer`.",
        "B": "Reversing inheritance still creates a false `is-a` claim; a hardware `Printer` is not a kind of `PrintQueue` in this model.",
        "D": "Derived classes can freely add private members in C++; the real flaw with public inheritance is exposing `Printer`'s interface on a non-`Printer` queue."
      },
      "difficulty_estimate": "medium",
      "misconception_tags": [
        "composition_vs_inheritance",
        "is_a_substitutability",
        "inheritance_for_delegation"
      ],
      "authorship_type": "AI-assisted team-authored",
      "reference_sources": [
        "https://isocpp.github.io/CppCoreGuidelines/CppCoreGuidelines#c120-use-class-hierarchies-to-represent-concepts-with-inherent-hierarchical-structure-only"
      ],
      "verification_method": "cpp17_compile_and_run",
      "agent_check_status": "passed",
      "human_review_status": "pending",
