      "topic_id": "OOP_T05_2",
      "learning_objective_id": "OOP_T05_2_LO02",
      "question_category": "misconception_analysis",
      "stem": "In this C++17 program, `Room` must keep `16 <= temp <= 30`, and the display must always show `Room`'s current temperature while wording can change independently. What does `s.show()` print, and which design fixes the mismatch?\n```cpp\n#include <iostream>\n\nclass Room {\nprivate:\n    int temp_;\npublic:\n    Room(int t) : temp_(t) {}\n    bool set_temp(int t) {\n        if (t < 16 || t > 30) return false;\n        temp_ = t;\n        return true;\n    }\n    int temp() const { return temp_; }\n};\n\nclass Screen {\nprivate:\n    int cached_temp_;\npublic:\n    Screen(const Room& r) : cached_temp_(r.temp()) {}\n    void show() const { std::cout << cached_temp_ << \"\\n\"; }\n};\n\nint main() {\n    Room r(20);\n    Screen s(r);\n    r.set_temp(24);\n    r.set_temp(40);\n    s.show();\n}\n```",
      "options": [
        {
          "option_id": "A",
          "text": "Prints `24`; keep `cached_temp_` in `Screen` and let `Room` read its temperature directly from `Screen`."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "B",
          "text": "Prints `40`; make `Screen` publicly inherit from `Room` so both objects share the same single `temp_` slot."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "C",
          "text": "Prints `20`; keep a separate writable copy in `Screen` and rely on callers to sync both fields manually."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "D",
          "text": "Prints `20`; let `Room` own the validated `temp_` and let a display helper read `r.temp()` each time it prints."
        }
      ],
      "correct_option_id": "D",
      "answer_explanation": "`Screen` copied `20` at construction; `set_temp(24)` updated `r`, `set_temp(40)` was rejected, and `s.show()` still prints stale `20`. Reading `r.temp()` via a helper keeps one authoritative value and lets display wording vary independently.",
      "distractor_explanations": {
        "A": "Students may assume constructor copying keeps values linked, yet `Screen` only copied `20` once, and `Screen` should not own `Room`'s validated state.",
        "B": "Learners sometimes use inheritance to share a field, but `40` is rejected by `Room` and inheriting creates a separate `Room` subobject, not a shared `Room`.",
        "C": "Stale output `20` is right, yet manual synchronization is fragile because any caller updating `Room` without updating `Screen` breaks the always-current display rule."
      },
      "difficulty_estimate": "medium",
      "misconception_tags": [
        "stale_duplicate_state",
        "inheritance_for_field_sharing",
        "invariant_ownership"
      ],
      "authorship_type": "AI-assisted team-authored",
      "reference_sources": [
        "https://isocpp.github.io/CppCoreGuidelines/CppCoreGuidelines#c2-use-class-if-the-class-has-an-invariant-use-struct-if-the-data-members-can-vary-independently",
        "https://isocpp.github.io/CppCoreGuidelines/CppCoreGuidelines#c4-make-a-function-a-member-only-if-it-needs-direct-access-to-the-representation-of-a-class",
        "https://isocpp.github.io/CppCoreGuidelines/CppCoreGuidelines#c120-use-class-hierarchies-to-represent-concepts-with-inherent-hierarchical-structure-only"
      ],
