      "question_category": "short_application",
      "stem": "Consider the following C++17 program where `parse_port` throws on invalid text and `load_port` owns the fallback policy:\n```cpp\n#include <iostream>\n#include <stdexcept>\n#include <string>\n\nint parse_port(const std::string& s) {\n    if (s.empty() || s[0] < '0' || s[0] > '9') {\n        throw std::invalid_argument(\"bad port\");\n    }\n    return std::stoi(s);\n}\n\nint load_port(const std::string& text) {\n    try {\n        return parse_port(text);\n    } catch (const std::invalid_argument&) {\n        return 8080;\n    }\n}\n\nint main() {\n    std::cout << load_port(\"abc\") << '\\n';\n    return 0;\n}\n```\nWhat does this program print, and why is `load_port` the right boundary?",
