      "topic_id": "OOP_T10_1",
      "learning_objective_id": "OOP_T10_1_LO02",
      "question_category": "short_application",
      "stem": "Consider the C++17 code below where `measure_elapsed()` calls `GlobalClock::instance()` directly:\n```cpp\n#include <iostream>\n\nclass GlobalClock {\nprivate:\n    int tick_ = 10;\n    GlobalClock() = default;\npublic:\n    GlobalClock(const GlobalClock&) = delete;\n    GlobalClock& operator=(const GlobalClock&) = delete;\n    static GlobalClock& instance() {\n        static GlobalClock clock;\n        return clock;\n    }\n    int now() const { return tick_; }\n    void advance(int delta) { tick_ += delta; }\n};\n\nint measure_elapsed() {\n    int start = GlobalClock::instance().now();\n    GlobalClock::instance().advance(5);\n    return GlobalClock::instance().now() - start;\n}\n\nint main() {\n    std::cout << measure_elapsed() << \"\\n\";\n}\n```\nA unit test wants to run `measure_elapsed()` with a controllable fake clock instead of the shared production `GlobalClock`. What design drawback makes this difficult?",
      "options": [
        {
          "option_id": "A",
          "text": "Marking `instance()` as `static` makes the function return a fresh clock copy on every call."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "B",
          "text": "Hardcoding `GlobalClock::instance()` couples the function to one non-replaceable global object."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "C",
          "text": "Deleting the copy constructor prevents `measure_elapsed()` from calling `now()` or `advance()`."
        },
        {
          "option_id": "D",
          "text": "Making the constructor `private` stops `GlobalClock::instance()` from creating `clock` locally."
        }
      ],
      "correct_option_id": "B",
      "answer_explanation": "Direct calls to `GlobalClock::instance()` hide and hardcode the dependency, making fake substitution in tests difficult.",
      "distractor_explanations": {
        "A": "Students sometimes misread `static` member functions as factories, whereas `static GlobalClock clock;` creates one persistent object shared across calls.",
        "C": "Learners may think `= delete` blocks all member access, but deleting copies only blocks duplicating `GlobalClock` while `GlobalClock::instance()` returns a usable reference.",
        "D": "Students forget that class access specifiers apply to outsiders; `GlobalClock::instance()` is a member function and can call the private constructor."
      },
