      "question_category": "misconception_analysis",
      "stem": "Look at the C++17 program below, which models two test setups configuring the same `MailService` sequentially:\n```cpp\n#include <iostream>\n#include <string>\n\nclass MailService {\nprivate:\n    std::string mode_ = \"prod\";\n    MailService() = default;\npublic:\n    MailService(const MailService&) = delete;\n    MailService& operator=(const MailService&) = delete;\n    static MailService& instance() {\n        static MailService service;\n        return service;\n    }\n    void set_mode(const std::string& m) { mode_ = m; }\n    std::string mode() const { return mode_; }\n};\n\nint main() {\n    MailService::instance().set_mode(\"mock1\");\n    MailService::instance().set_mode(\"mock2\");\n    std::cout << MailService::instance().mode() << \"\\n\";\n}\n```\nWhat testing limitation does this output (`mock2`) illustrate when two tests need separate mock `MailService` objects?",
